<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/intermediate/political-philosophy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    States demand taxes, obedience and — sometimes — your life. What could make that
    <em>legitimate</em> rather than mere force? The classic answer, running from the seventeenth
    century to today, is the <strong>social contract</strong>: political authority is justified
    by some agreement — real or hypothetical — among the people who live under it. The
    philosophers disagree spectacularly about what life is like before government, and the
    disagreement decides everything downstream.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the contract conversation, 1651–1971 — order-first vs rights-first vs fairness-first" widget="timeline" config={contractTimeline} />

  <p>
    Hobbes, scarred by civil war, feared anarchy more than tyranny and licensed near-absolute
    sovereigns. Locke, scarred differently, said rebellion is not just permitted but <em>duty</em>
    when rulers become predators — an argument Jefferson copied nearly verbatim into the
    Declaration of Independence. Rousseau moved sovereignty into "the people" collectively.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="Rawls's veil of ignorance — the contract argued in four steps" widget="equationStep" config={veilCfg} />

  <h2>The standing objections</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No one signed.</strong> Critics from Hume (1753) onward note no historical contract exists; defenders answer that the contract is a <em>test</em> of legitimacy, not a historical event.</li>
    <li><strong>Who counts as "the people"?</strong> Contract theorists were slow to include women (Carole Pateman's critique, 1988) or the colonised (Charles Mills, 1997). The veil is younger than it feels.</li>
    <li><strong>Consent fades.</strong> If legitimacy comes from agreement, what about citizens born into the deal? Every generation is, in Hume's phrase, "months old" at contract time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Still, the contract tradition owns the question modern politics keeps asking: <em>what
    could free and equal people possibly agree to?</em> When constitutions are written or
    rewritten, this is the argument they are having.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hobbes, Leviathan (1651); Locke, Two Treatises (1689); Rousseau (1762); Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971).</p>
</TopicPage>
